          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does acid reflux tend to happen after meals?",
          "answer": "Acid reflux often occurs after meals due to several factors related to how the stomach and esophagus function. One primary reason is that large or heavy meals can increase stomach pressure and slow stomach emptying, allowing acid to back up into the esophagus. Additionally, certain foods and drinks, particularly those high in fat, alcohol, or carbonation, can contribute by relaxing the valve between the stomach and esophagus, enhancing the likelihood of reflux.\n\nThe symptoms are commonly described as heartburn, where a burning feeling rises behind the breastbone, and regurgitation of acid or food, which is generally worse after meals, bending forward, or lying down (source: Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)). \n\nI could not provide detailed mechanisms of the esophageal or gastric physiology that leads to these issues or specific dietary recommendations.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
